{
  "id": "tetris-dx-1998",
  "kind": "game",
  "title": "Tetris DX",
  "subtitle": "Nintendo · Game Boy Color",
  "summary": "Tetris DX is Nintendo's Game Boy Color version of the falling-block puzzle, released with the new handheld generation in Japan in 1998. Tetrominoes still have to be rotated and arranged into complete horizontal lines before the growing stack reaches the top. Colour helps distinguish their shapes, while saved profiles and additional modes give repeated sessions a longer record. The game preserves the immediate clarity of the original portable Tetris, then changes the surrounding goals so that the same arrangement skills can be tested in several different ways.",
  "body": [
    "Tetris DX is Nintendo's Game Boy Color version of the falling-block puzzle, released with the new handheld generation in Japan in 1998. Tetrominoes still have to be rotated and arranged into complete horizontal lines before the growing stack reaches the top. Colour helps distinguish their shapes, while saved profiles and additional modes give repeated sessions a longer record. The game preserves the immediate clarity of the original portable Tetris, then changes the surrounding goals so that the same arrangement skills can be tested in several different ways.",
    "Marathon follows the familiar continuing game, while 40 Lines asks how quickly a fixed target can be reached. Ultra instead gives the player two minutes to create the strongest result. These limits change how the well is managed: a safe long-term structure and a fast short attempt do not always ask for the same placement. Versus play offers computer opponents or another person through the Game Link Cable, turning the puzzle into a direct contest. Each mode gives the next piece a different practical context.",
    "Up to four profiles record names, high scores and performance information, allowing several people to build their own history on the cartridge. The convenience makes another short attempt feel connected to what was achieved before. Nintendo developed the game around preserving Tetris's core rather than adding a complicated replacement for it. The result suits the handheld's daily use: the rule can be understood immediately, while a saved target or a different time limit supplies another reason to return after the screen has been switched off."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "The familiar well acquires more ways to play",
    "Forty lines or two minutes",
    "A score that survives the power switch"
  ],
